---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, PLATFORM_PAGES } from '../i18n/routes';
import { PLATFORMS, PLATFORM_UI, type PlatformKey } from '../i18n/platforms';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'platforms' as const;
const pf = PLATFORM_UI[lang];
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Supported platforms"
  description="GitHub, Azure DevOps and GitLab. See which hosts are covered on each platform, where the extension stops, and which browsers it installs in.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g18 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow acc">compatibility:</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(29px,3.8vw,41px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.031em;line-height:1.05">Supported platforms</h1>
          <p class="lede">The extension works on three code review platforms. Each card lists the hosts it covers; the page of each platform details its features, its setup and its limitations.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="avail">
          {(PLATFORM_PAGES as PlatformKey[]).map((key) => {
            const p = PLATFORMS[key];
            return (
              <div class="av-card">
                <span class:list={['av-tag', p.support === 'full' ? 'ok' : 'part']}>{pf.support[p.support]}</span>
                <h3>{p.name}</h3>
                <ul class="av-list">
                  {p.covered.map((h) => <li>{h.name} {h.note && <span class="av-note">{h.note[lang]}</span>}</li>)}
                </ul>
                <a class="av-more" href={href(key, lang)}>{pf.details}</a>
              </div>
            );
          })}
        </div>

        <div class="stack g12">
          <span class="eyebrow">and on your machine:</span>
          <h2 style="font-size:20px">Supported browsers</h2>
          <ul class="av-browsers">
            <li><b>Chrome, Edge, Brave</b> <span class="av-note">and other Chromium-based browsers</span></li>
            <li><b>Firefox</b> <span class="av-note">separate build</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">not covered:</span>
          <h2>Other platforms</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The convention itself works anywhere — it's simply a way of writing comments. What exists only on the platforms above is the tooling: the toolbar, live validation, the badges and the banner that counts blocking threads.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="avail">
          <div class="av-card soon">
            <span class="av-tag">out of scope</span>
            <h3>Bitbucket and Gerrit</h3>
            <p class="av-p">Neither is in the toolkit's scope today.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="av-card soon">
            <span class="av-tag">tell us</span>
            <h3>Need another platform?</h3>
            <p class="av-p">Open an issue on the repository and tell us which platform you use and roughly how many people would benefit from it. We use that information to decide what to support next.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="small measure">We don't promise dates. A platform is only listed as available once it actually works.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="split">
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">what you need:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">The extension needs no infrastructure.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Install it, allow the domains where you review, and it works: the toolbar, shortcuts, and live feedback. No account, no server, no configuration required. The store listings haven't been published yet, so for now the extension is installed from a release archive. The installation page provides browser-specific instructions.</p>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The extension is the whole product: there is no server to deploy and no check to add to a pipeline. It reports unresolved blocking threads and never blocks a merge. A team that wants merges to wait for resolved conversations uses its platform's own setting for that.</p>
            <div class="hero-btns">
              <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
              <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('adoption', 'en')}>How teams adopt it</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">and where it runs:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">Only the domains you allow.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The extension doesn't request access to every site. A fresh install has access to none. You grant the extension access to each domain one at a time: your browser asks for that permission, you choose whether to grant it, and you can withdraw it afterwards from the extension's own options page.</p>
            <p class="small">Two permissions in total: one to remember your preferences and one to work inside the page. No account. No stored token. The extension cannot load code from anywhere else. That restriction is enforced technically and verified again with every release.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>If your platform is listed, you can start today.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">Nothing becomes blocking until your team decides it should.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('labels', 'en')}>See the labels</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
